  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nShower/Storm Chances Tonight into Saturday...Dry conditions will \ncontinue into the early evening hours, with only a stray shower or\nstorm possible near Vilas Co. Then, higher chances for showers \nand storms will spread into central and northern WI (40-70%) late \nthis evening as a shortwave and cold front advance across WI. The \nactivity will outrun the better instability in the late evening,\nwith any instability over northern WI waning after midnight, so\nthe severe threat looks very low, but strong winds aloft could\nlead to some gusty winds as the line weakens. Not much rain \nexpected with this activity either, with most spots seeing under a\nquarter inch. By the time the initial area gets into eastern WI, \nnot much will be left other than maybe some sprinkles. Then as the\nfront completes its passage, a shower or storm will be possible \nacross far eastern WI through around noon, but forcing will be \nlimited and a weak cap will remain in place, so have only included\nsome slight chance PoPs over Door Co. Weak high pressure is then \nforecast to move into the western Great Lakes bringing mainly dry \nconditions for the remainder of the weekend. However, another weak\nsystem could graze the southern counties with some clouds or \nshowers on Sunday.\n\nHeavy Rain and Storm Potential Next Week...Ensemble and\ndeterministic models in fairly good agreement showing a strong low\npressure system and shortwave tracking across the northern Plains\nearly next week, which will usher in PWATs up to 2\" (near the 99th\npercentile of climatology). As the LLJ cranks up on Monday, a\nwidespread area of showers and storms will form to our southwest\nand move into the area Monday afternoon into Tuesday morning, with\nthe exact timing still to be ironed out. Severe weather threat\nlooks low due to limited instability and a strong capping\ninversion, but elevated instability and the mean flow also \nbriefly becomes more parallel with the boundary layer flow which \nwill promote training convection. This could lead to some heavy \nrain and a soaking rain for many spots. Question is just how long \nit will sit over our area and how many rounds of rain any one\nlocation will see. NBM probs have lowered a little, down to \n30-45% chance for greater than 2\" of rain Monday into early \nTuesday, with the highest chances across central WI. WPC has \nalso shifted a Slight Chance for excessive rain a little to the\nsouth. Will still need to monitor for heavy rain with the high \nPWATs and possible training convection, but will not hit the \nmessaging too hard at this point and watch for trends. \n\nRest of the Forecast...questions remain on how far south the\nboundary will get next week within the zonal flow over the Great\nLakes and if we will hit with additional showers and storms or if\nhigh pressure wins out and we stay mainly dry. NBM brings low end \nPoPs (20-40%), especially across the south, for much of the week, \nwhich is basically just portraying the differences in location of \nthe boundary. Hopefully the picture gets a little clearer as we \nget closer. If we were to get more bouts of heavy rain later in \nthe week, this could increase the overall flood threat and will \nneed to be monitored. Some cooler air will filter into the region \nbehind the system on Tuesday, with some chilly air in store if the\nhigh wins out.",
